course: dictionary
lesson: 14686
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Presumption can mean an idea you accept as true without proof, or rude, too-confident behaviour. In law, it can also mean an idea a court treats as true until someone proves otherwise.
explain: «We made a presumption that the bus would be late, because it was snowing.» Nobody has checked yet, so this is a belief we accept for now. That is the first meaning: believing something is true without proof.
explain: «The law protects everyone with the presumption of innocence.» Here presumption is a rule a court starts from. The word «innocence» and the word «law» are your clues, so this is the legal meaning.
explain: «What presumption, to take the best seat without asking!» The words «What» and the exclamation mark show that someone is annoyed. Presumption here means rude, too-confident behaviour, doing something you have no right to do. Ask yourself: does the sentence sound cheeky or angry? Then it is the third meaning.
fact: Presumption is a noun: a presumption, presumptions.
fact: «Presumption» means something you believe is true without proof.
fact: In law, «the presumption of innocence» means a person is treated as innocent until proved guilty.
fact: «Presumption» can also mean rude, too-confident behaviour, like doing something you have no right to do.
fact: Check your meaning by swapping in «guess» or «belief». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the first meaning.
exercise: «We made the presumption that the shop was open, so we walked there.» What does presumption mean here? | *Something believed to be true without proof | Rude, too-confident behaviour | A very large box | A court rule that protects everyone
exercise: «The presumption of innocence protects everyone in court.» Find one word that tells you this sentence is about law. | presumption | protects | *court | everyone
exercise: Ella says, «What presumption, to take the best seat without asking!» What does presumption mean? | A kind gift | *Rude, too-confident behaviour | A sleepy feeling | Proof that something is true
test: «On the presumption that the match is cancelled, we stayed home.» What does presumption mean here? | A fine paid in court | A proven fact from a scientist | *Something believed to be true without proof | Something believed without proof
test: «Her presumption made the teacher frown: she answered for everyone and ignored the rules.» What does presumption mean here? | A court rule that protects everyone | *Rude, too-confident behaviour | Something believed without proof | A science experiment
exam: «In maths, a presumption must be checked before we trust it.» What does this sentence tell us about a presumption? | It is a kind of food | *It is a belief that must be checked | It is always right and never needs checking | It means rude behaviour in class
